[{:db/id 17592186166855 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e4d96c0e-efbf-5a8a-a19d-a5214c9f4f1b" "question-bbf273f7-9d05-5a98-8977-1ce41a23dc4d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166708 :question/problem "Given that $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}\\right),$ what is $\\textrm{E}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Exp}({\\lambda})$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X \\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}\\right),$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] =\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}\\right)}=\\sqrt 3\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166868 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3ab14871-8f29-5705-a194-c2697a586708"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166721 :question/problem "The time $X,$ in hours, between two customers ordering a chocolate muffin at a pastry shop can be modeled as an exponential random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(6).$ If a customer has just ordered a chocolate muffin, what is the average time, in minutes, until the next customer orders a chocolate muffin?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X \\sim \\textrm{Exp}({\\lambda})$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X \\sim \\textrm{Exp}\\left(6\\right).$ Therefore, $\\lambda = 6,$ which means that $6$ customers order a chocolate muffin each hour. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] =\\dfrac{1}{6}\\, \\textrm{h} = 10 \\, \\textrm{min}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average time until the next customer orders a chocolate muffin is $10$ minutes." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166856 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-906813e4-c59b-59ab-b35c-7a3a5c47ebad" "question-5e5fad29-e988-5832-87d5-889a9c3d0b32"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166709 :question/problem "Given that $X\\sim  \\textrm{Exp}\\left(0.1\\right),$ what is $\\textrm{SD}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X \\sim  \\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SD}[X] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[X]} = \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X \\sim  \\textrm{Exp}\\left(0.1\\right),$ we have the following standard deviation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\frac{1}{(0.1)} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-e4d96c0e-efbf-5a8a-a19d-a5214c9f4f1b" :question/id #uuid "e4d96c0e-efbf-5a8a-a19d-a5214c9f4f1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-132345" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(9),$ what is $\\text{E}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(9),$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{9}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-491048e3-2299-5fa0-8450-dfa6f0ac7b8c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-491048e3-2299-5fa0-8450-dfa6f0ac7b8c" :answer-field/id #uuid "491048e3-2299-5fa0-8450-dfa6f0ac7b8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b30c5443-9c3e-5c06-92b7-a5da84135144" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dcf023c7-3049-55e1-a4a8-ae6d93f4c21a" "answer-ac1ddfe8-3a5c-5829-ba63-8ede3e37e56b" "answer-e1e0617c-de3a-557e-b0c7-efefa5260d20" "answer-b30c5443-9c3e-5c06-92b7-a5da84135144" "answer-06354ca2-a391-54fa-91a5-83326d62cbc4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcf023c7-3049-55e1-a4a8-ae6d93f4c21a" :answer/id #uuid "dcf023c7-3049-55e1-a4a8-ae6d93f4c21a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac1ddfe8-3a5c-5829-ba63-8ede3e37e56b" :answer/id #uuid "ac1ddfe8-3a5c-5